%n enkīdu | ibiršu || īpuš | libba
#note: See @bib{RN117@265}: “The decipherment of the line’s verb as @i{īpuš} makes it difficult to retain a literal translation of @i{libbam} (e.g. Dalley ‘struck at (?) the heart’, Pettinato ‘trafisse il cuore’, and Hecker ‘@i{traf} sein Herz’). With Lambert I understand the phrase @i{libbam epēšum} to mean ‘give encouragement’ (cf. his translation of ll. 19'–23' in @bib{RN425@42}), comparable with the well-known idiom @i{libbam šakānum}, ‘to hearten, encourage, comfort’. However, the late version, though badly damaged at this point, gives Enkidu a more physical role and expands the line to include the lungs (SB V 265): @i{En}[@i{kīdu libba}? @i{il}]@i{putu adi ḫašê ištalpu}.”
// cf. L I.4 SB "V" 288

%n ištū-ma | sebet | inēru
#note: See @bib{RN117@266}: “A subordinating conjunction is unlikely to appear in the middle of a verse, so it seems that this long line must be two lines of poetry: @i{ḫepûtim ra…} [x] / @i{ištūma sebet inēru}. Otherwise one must read @i{iš-ṭú-ma ... inērū}, ‘they spread out and slew’. I prefer to keep the verbs singular, like those of the following lines. Seven is the number of Ḫuwawa’s auras, of course, so that this line concludes the slaughter of his household.”
// cf. L I.4 SB "V" 307

%n urid-ma | irtahi<ṣ> | qištam
#note: See @bib{RN117@266}: “It seems that here also two poetic lines are squeezed on to one line of tablet. (…)”
// cf. L I.4 OB "IM" 17

%n mūšab (|) anunnakkī || puzzuram | ipte
#note: See @bib{RN117@266}: “(…) The alternative to the ‘sandhi’ writing proposed by Bauer (= @i{puzzuram ipte}), is to read @i{pu-sú-um-mi ip-te}, ‘he opened the veils’ (@bib{parpola_standard_1997@238}; cf. already Pettinato, Tournay and Shaffer, Westenholz). The objections to this analysis are that (a) @i{pusummum} seems to be only a garment, not a curtain of the kind used in temples (which is @i{šiddum}), and (b) with no preposition before @i{mūšab ilī}, @i{ipte} would be forced to govern two objects. In either analysis the image is of the violation of the most sacred part of a sanctuary, which is normally hidden from human gaze. The tradition in which the mountain of the Cedar Forest is the home of the gods is not native to Babylonia; see Chapter 10, the introduction to SB V.”
// cf. L I.4 OB "IM" 18

%n gilgāmeš | iṣṣī | ubattaq | enkīdu | uharra | urbazillī
#note: See @bib{RN117@266}: “I read @i{urbazillī} (Bauer: @i{ur-ma-zi-li}; see @i{AHw} s.v. @i{urmazillu}) in the light of the later text, which has @i{hur-ba-zal-lu} (…), presumably a form of the same word. The customary understanding of the last two words is that Enkidu was digging up the tree-stumps. Tree-stumps are not useful as lumber, and now that the later text has the verb @i{ištene”â} it is clear that in this line @i{ú-ha-ra} is from @i{hiʾārum} (@i{uharra}, ventive) not @i{herûm}. The II/1 stem, previously unattested for @i{ḫiʾārum}, suits the plurality of the object the verb governs. As I see it, while Gilgameš fells one tree Enkidu is busy choosing the next (cf. already Tournay and Shaffer: ‘Enkidu choisit une bille de bois rare’).”
// cf. L I.4 SB "V" 311-312
